<description>
Recolor every large solid monochrome rectangle hidden in the noisy grid to yellow.

Input structure
A 2D grid with multicolor noise and no pre-existing yellow pixels; the meaningful
objects are one or more axis-aligned solid rectangles of a single color.

Rule
Find all maximal same-color rectangles whose height and width are both at least
4, and recolor exactly those rectangle cells to yellow.

Key insight
The hidden objects are the only large uniform rectangular regions; maximality
excludes smaller subrectangles inside them while the side-length threshold
ignores ordinary short runs in the noise.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "maximal monochromatic rectangular regions with height and width at least 4 in the noisy grid",
  "transform": "recolor every cell of each such rectangle to yellow; preserve all other pixels"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 30x30 [observed]
  palette: {1, 2, 3, 6, 8} -- no yellow (4) in input
  noise:
    fill: iid random from the 5 palette colors
    constraint: no 3x3 or larger uniform block may exist outside the hidden rectangles
  rectangles:
    count: 2-4 non-overlapping axis-aligned solid monochromatic rectangles
    height: 4-7
    width: 5-10
    color: each rectangle uses a distinct color from the palette [observed]
    placement: fully interior to grid (min 2-cell gap from every edge [observed]); pairwise non-overlapping
  output:
    same as input but every cell belonging to a maximal uniform rectangle (h>=4, w>=4) is recolored to yellow (4)

generation_strategy:
  approach: output-first -- place rectangles first, then fill noise around them
  steps:
    1. Choose grid 30x30, pick 2-4 rectangle specs (height 4-7, width 5-10), each with a distinct color from {1,2,3,6,8}.
    2. Place rectangles on the grid with >=2-cell margin from edges, ensuring no overlap.
    3. Fill every non-rectangle cell with iid random choice from {1,2,3,6,8}.
    4. Rejection-check noise region for 3x3+ uniform blocks; if any exist, re-randomize those cells until none remain (locally re-sample the offending cells).
    5. Output is a copy of the grid with all rectangle cells set to yellow (4).
```
</generation_spec>
</reference_puzzle>

